# Reset and Clock Timing

## Power-On Reset (POR)
The power-on reset module in [rtl/por.sv](mdc:rtl/por.sv) provides a clean reset signal:

```systemverilog
module por(input bit clk, input bit reset, output bit user_reset);
  // Longer counter for simulation to ensure full reset sequence completes
  logic [31:0] counter = 32'hFFFFFFFF;
  initial user_reset = 1;
  
  // Reset sequence logic
  always_ff @(posedge clk)
  begin
    if (reset) begin 
      // External reset restarts the counter
      counter <= 32'hFFFFFFFF;    
      user_reset <= 1;
    end else if (counter == 0) begin
      // Reset sequence completed
      user_reset <= 0;
    end else begin
      counter <= counter - 1;
    end
  end
endmodule
```

## Clock Generation
The clock module [rtl/clocks.sv](mdc:rtl/clocks.sv) generates multiple clock domains:

```systemverilog
module clocks(input bit clk, output bit reset, output bit masterclk, output bit videoclk, output bit cpuclk);
  // Use a much shorter reset period for simulation
  logic [15:0] reset_counter = 16'hFFFF;  // Start with reset active
  
  // Reset generation logic
  assign reset = (reset_counter != 0);  // Reset active during counter period
  assign masterclk = clk;
  assign videoclk = clk;
  assign cpuclk = clk;
endmodule
```

## Clock Domain Considerations
- For simulation, using a single clock domain simplifies debugging
- When using multiple clock domains:
  1. Ensure proper synchronization at domain crossings
  2. Register data at both ends of a crossing
  3. Use clock-enable logic instead of gated clocks

## Reset Sequence Debug
- The POR module provides debug messages at key points
- The clocks module displays reset progress
- CPU module shows vector reads during reset
- Check for proper propagation of reset to all components
